<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_let.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-let">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-let">
  <primary>LET</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>LET</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>LET</refname>
  <refpurpose>add computed values to the current row</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
LET <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> = <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> = <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> ] ...
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-let-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>LET</command> introduces one or more new variables into the current row. Each one
   is computed from an expression over the variables that are already in scope.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>LET</command> is additive. Every binding in scope stays in scope, so
   <command>LET</command> can never drop a variable from the pipeline. That is what separates
   it from <xref linkend="cypher-with"/>, which projects only the items it lists. The
   equivalent <command>WITH</command> form is
   <literal>WITH *, <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> AS <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Each variable is introduce only. A name that is already in scope cannot be rebound. That
   holds inside the body of a subquery expression, and inside an importing
   <literal>CALL { }</literal>, as much as it holds at the top level.
  </para>

  <para>
   Expressions are evaluated once per input row. Aggregates are not allowed.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>LET</command> may open a query or sit in the middle of one. It cannot end one. It
   is the clause to open a statement with, because <command>WITH</command> in that position is
   SQL's common table expression rather than a Cypher projection.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-let-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the new binding. It must not already be in scope. It is visible to every clause
      that follows.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Any value expression over the current row: property access, arithmetic, a function call,
      <literal>CASE</literal>, a list constructor or a map constructor. It is evaluated once
      per input row.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-let-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Add a derived value and keep everything else in scope:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)
LET age_in_2026 = 2026 - p.born
RETURN p.name AS name, age_in_2026
ORDER BY age_in_2026 LIMIT 3;

       name        | age_in_2026
-------------------+-------------
 "Ryan Gosling"    | 46
 "Neill Blomkamp"  | 47
 "Sam Worthington" | 50
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The <command>WITH</command> form returns the same rows:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)
WITH *, 2026 - p.born AS age_in_2026
RETURN p.name AS name, age_in_2026
ORDER BY age_in_2026 LIMIT 3;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The difference is what happens to <literal>p</literal>. <command>LET</command> keeps it
   without being asked. The <command>WITH</command> form has to ask for it with
   <literal>*</literal>, and a <command>WITH</command> that leaves the <literal>*</literal> out
   drops <literal>p</literal>, so the <command>RETURN</command> then fails.
  </para>

  <para>
   Because <command>LET</command> is additive it can open a statement, seeding values with
   nothing yet in scope:
<programlisting>
LET radius = 7, area = 3.14159 * 7 * 7
RETURN radius, area;

 radius |   area
--------+-----------
 7      | 153.93791
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A name that is already in scope cannot be rebound:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) LET p = 1 RETURN p;

ERROR:  variable "p" already exists
HINT:  LET cannot redefine an existing variable; use a different name.
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-let-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <literal>let</literal> became a reserved keyword in 2.18. A database that used
   <literal>let</literal> as a label name or as a variable name under an earlier version has to
   quote it as <literal>"let"</literal>, with double quotes rather than backticks, or rename
   it. Property keys and <command>RETURN</command> aliases are not affected.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-let-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>LET</command> implements the GQL LET statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, section
   14.7. Variable definition, several bindings in one clause, the leading position and
   expressions evaluated per row are all supported. A <command>LET</command> over an aggregate
   expression is not.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher has no <command>LET</command> statement. The equivalent there is
   <literal>WITH *, <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> AS <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></literal>,
   which AgensGraph accepts unchanged.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

